WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.111 --> 00:00:12.121
<v Daniel>Everyone thinks the ATM killed the bank dealer, right? Like it's one of those bits of common knowledge that just feels instinctively true. You introduce a machine that spits out cash and well, you obviously don't need a human to hand out cash anymore.</v>

2
00:00:12.241 --> 00:00:21.228
<v Maya>Right. Which would make perfect sense if the job was just, you know, a single monolithic thing. But the reality of what actually happened in banking is a lot stranger.</v>

3
00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:29.904
<v Daniel>Because after the ATM rolled out across the country, banks didn't fire all their tellers. They actually ended up hiring more of them, which is wild.</v>

4
00:00:29.964 --> 00:00:38.688
<v Maya>It is wild. And that paradox, the machine taking over, but the human still being in demand, that is the skeleton key to understanding the future of work.</v>

5
00:00:39.069 --> 00:00:53.518
<v Daniel>Yeah, we are constantly trying to diagnose how technology is reshaping what humans do all day. And if you are in the higher education space right now, especially in a massive public urban system like CUNY here in New York, you are staring down the barrel of this.</v>

6
00:00:53.778 --> 00:00:54.418
<v Maya>Oh, absolutely.</v>

7
00:00:54.918 --> 00:01:09.806
<v Daniel>You're tasked with preparing people structurally for a future that is just shifting under their feet. We can't rely on vague warnings about like robots taking jobs. We need the mechanics. We need to know what happens inside the engine of the economy.</v>

8
00:01:10.268 --> 00:01:24.477
<v Maya>To do that, we have to go back to the bedrock. So today we're doing a deep dive into a classic. It's a highly influential June 2001 MIT working paper by economists David Autor, Frank Levy, and Richard Murnane.</v>

9
00:01:24.777 --> 00:01:25.797
<v Daniel>Right, the ALM paper.</v>

10
00:01:26.138 --> 00:01:31.181
<v Maya>Exactly. In the field, it's almost always just called the ALM paper, and it is foundational.</v>

11
00:01:31.747 --> 00:01:45.977
<v Daniel>But we're not just reading history today, though. We are going to take this 2001 classic and pressure test it against five modern sources. We're marching all the way up to 2026 data to see if its core idea still holds up in the era of artificial intelligence.</v>

12
00:01:46.338 --> 00:01:56.142
<v Maya>Yeah, because we really have to start with ALM. It taught economists a radical new concept, which is that a job... is not a title. A job is a bundle of tasks.</v>

13
00:01:56.222 --> 00:01:57.283
<v Daniel>A bundle of tasks.</v>

14
00:01:57.443 --> 00:02:12.194
<v Maya>You're right. That distinction changes everything. I mean, before this paper, the default setting for an economist was to look at employment through the lens of a job title. You are a machinist or a secretary. But ALM argued that to understand automation, you have to break the</v>

15
00:02:12.234 --> 00:02:15.457
<v Maya>title open and look at the discrete tasks inside.</v>

16
00:02:15.938 --> 00:02:21.542
<v Daniel>Okay. Let's unpack this. I like to think of a job title as like a box of Lego bricks.</v>

17
00:02:21.702 --> 00:02:22.343
<v Maya>Oh, I like that.</v>

18
00:02:22.463 --> 00:02:34.933
<v Daniel>Yeah. So the outside of the box, the label printed on it, like bank teller, that stays exactly the same decade after decade. But the specific bricks inside that box, the actual daily tasks, they are constantly being swapped out.</v>

19
00:02:35.173 --> 00:02:36.354
<v Maya>Yes, exactly.</v>

20
00:02:36.514 --> 00:02:48.238
<v Daniel>You take out the routine cash counting brick and you put in a non-routine customer relationship management brick. But to understand why those bricks get swapped out, we have to look at what computers are fundamentally built to do, right?</v>

21
00:02:49.098 --> 00:02:57.983
<v Maya>At their very core, early computers and software programs executed procedural rules-based logic. The authors call it if-then-do logic.</v>

22
00:02:58.043 --> 00:02:58.583
<v Daniel>If-then-do.</v>

23
00:02:58.724 --> 00:03:06.428
<v Maya>Yeah. You give a machine a fully specified, unambiguous sequence of commands, and it executes them flawlessly millions of times a second.</v>

24
00:03:07.032 --> 00:03:13.337
<v Daniel>So traditional coding is basically giving a computer a painfully strict recipe. And if the recipe isn't perfect, the cake fails.</v>

25
00:03:13.817 --> 00:03:22.324
<v Maya>That's a great way to look at it. And because computers demand that strict recipe, the ALM paper argues they naturally substitute for routine tasks.</v>

26
00:03:22.604 --> 00:03:26.447
<v Daniel>Meaning things that can be easily proceduralized or written down step by step.</v>

27
00:03:26.788 --> 00:03:35.995
<v Maya>Precisely. But crucially, they complement non-routine tasks. Things like complex problem solving, abstract thinking, and, you know, interactive communication.</v>

28
00:03:36.375 --> 00:03:47.861
<v Daniel>Which led the authors to build this incredible two by two matrix to categorize all human labor. You have routine versus non-routine on one axis and cognitive versus manual on the other.</v>

29
00:03:48.382 --> 00:03:53.144
<v Maya>Yeah. And when you start mapping jobs onto that matrix, the vulnerability to automation becomes instantly clear.</v>

30
00:03:53.344 --> 00:03:54.545
<v Daniel>Walk us through an example of that.</v>

31
00:03:55.109 --> 00:04:06.779
<v Maya>Sure. Let's take a routine cognitive task like double entry bookkeeping. In the 1970s, you hired a high school graduate to do this. It required brainpower, but it followed strict, unbending mathematical rules.</v>

32
00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:14.686
<v Daniel>So a computer running basic accounting software comes along and can completely substitute for that human labor. It's the perfect if-then-do scenario.</v>

33
00:04:14.807 --> 00:04:29.336
<v Maya>Exactly. Now, contrast that with a non-routine cognitive task, like a lawyer writing a persuasive legal brief. A computer in the 1990s could never write that brief. I mean, it requires drawing subtle inferences, forming arguments, understanding human</v>

34
00:04:29.416 --> 00:04:34.219
<v Maya>nuance and emotional weight. There is no strict mathematical rulebook for persuasion.</v>

35
00:04:34.239 --> 00:04:43.505
<v Daniel>That makes total sense. And we see this exact same divide in the manual domain, too, like bank tellers and clerks perform highly routine manual and cognitive tasks.</v>

36
00:04:43.932 --> 00:04:48.876
<v Maya>Right. Sorting letters by zip code, logging transactions, very easily substituted.</v>

37
00:04:49.357 --> 00:04:59.606
<v Daniel>But I have to push back on something here because the contrast is wild when you really think about it. In the 1990s, we had computers that could calculate infinite chess moves and beat grandmasters.</v>

38
00:04:59.926 --> 00:05:00.587
<v Maya>Yeah, deep blue.</v>

39
00:05:00.847 --> 00:05:09.955
<v Daniel>Right. And chess is infinitely more complicated than, say, driving a truck down I-95. Yet early computers couldn't drive a truck. Why?</v>

40
00:05:10.536 --> 00:05:17.722
<v Maya>That discrepancy is actually the philosophical limit of early automation. It brings us to a concept called Polanyi's paradox.</v>

41
00:05:18.082 --> 00:05:19.383
<v Daniel>Polanyi's paradox.</v>

42
00:05:19.583 --> 00:05:28.329
<v Maya>Yeah. Michael Polanyi was a philosopher who made this profoundly simple observation. He said, we do not know how to do many of the things we do.</v>

43
00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:32.554
<v Daniel>We do not know how to do the things we do. That's heavy.</v>

44
00:05:33.075 --> 00:05:38.978
<v Maya>It is. Think about a toddler walking across a messy living room to pick up an apple from a table.</v>

45
00:05:39.318 --> 00:05:39.578
<v Daniel>Okay.</v>

46
00:05:40.078 --> 00:05:53.905
<v Maya>That child is not consciously calculating optical recognition algorithms. They aren't running mathematical models on the bipedal mechanics of navigating in an uneven floor or, you know, adjusting their grip strength for the weight of the apple.</v>

47
00:05:54.206 --> 00:05:54.766
<v Daniel>They just do it.</v>

48
00:05:55.006 --> 00:05:58.368
<v Maya>They just do it. It relies entirely on tacit knowledge.</v>

49
00:05:58.768 --> 00:06:13.724
<v Daniel>It's the Lego in the dark problem. Try writing a strict, rule-based software algorithm for a human foot to dynamically avoid stepping on a Lego brick in a pitch-black hallway. You just can't write the code. Because we don't even know how</v>

50
00:06:13.804 --> 00:06:17.108
<v Daniel>our own brains process that chaotic physical data.</v>

51
00:06:17.832 --> 00:06:32.578
<v Maya>Driving a truck in heavy traffic relies on that exact same tacit, unspoken knowledge. Because programmers in the 1990s couldn't write down the explicit if-then-do rules for driving, they couldn't automate it. Polanyi's paradox formed a massive protective</v>

52
00:06:32.638 --> 00:06:34.699
<v Maya>wall around non-routine manual work.</v>

53
00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:49.046
<v Daniel>Okay, so we have this theory. Computers are walled off from non-routine work by Polanyi's paradox, but they are absolutely unleashed on routine work. What was the economic trigger that actually set this machine in motion?</v>

54
00:06:49.286 --> 00:06:49.746
<v Maya>What do you mean?</v>

55
00:06:50.286 --> 00:06:55.988
<v Daniel>Well, it couldn't just be that computers existed, right? They had a big financial sense for businesses to adopt them.</v>

56
00:06:56.408 --> 00:07:08.012
<v Maya>Ah, right. The trigger was an unprecedented collapse in price. The ALM paper cites a staggering number to anchor their model. The price of computer capital fell by about 35% a year from 1985 to 1996.</v>

57
00:07:11.698 --> 00:07:12.739
<v Daniel>35% a year.</v>

58
00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:14.341
<v Maya>That is a free fall.</v>

59
00:07:14.661 --> 00:07:29.314
<v Daniel>It completely changed the economic math for every business in America. What's fascinating here is how this became a massive causal force. It drove the wages of routine tasks down and shrank routine employment. Because why pay a human to do</v>

60
00:07:29.374 --> 00:07:31.696
<v Daniel>bookkeeping when the software is practically giving itself away?</v>

61
00:07:32.056 --> 00:07:46.543
<v Maya>Exactly. But here is the secondary complementary effect. Because computers were taking over the routine, tedious work, they raised the productivity, and therefore the economic value, of workers doing non-routine problem solving.</v>

62
00:07:47.503 --> 00:07:55.527
<v Daniel>So the machine offloads the routine stuff, which frees up the human to do the high-level interactive work, making that human infinitely more productive.</v>

63
00:07:55.787 --> 00:07:55.967
<v Maya>Yes.</v>

64
00:07:56.705 --> 00:08:11.669
<v Daniel>The theory makes total logical sense. But how do you actually measure this? Like, if the title on the Lego box stays the same, but the tasks inside are shifting invisibly, how did ALM prove it? You can't just look at standard government labor data</v>

65
00:08:11.689 --> 00:08:13.590
<v Daniel>and say, ah, yes, the tasks are changing.</v>

66
00:08:14.230 --> 00:08:24.813
<v Maya>That was the true methodological breakthrough of the Otter, Levy, and Murnane paper. They found a way to quantify the invisible. They turned to the Dictionary of Occupational Titles, or the DOT.</v>

67
00:08:25.359 --> 00:08:29.401
<v Daniel>No, for those who haven't dug into labor archives, this isn't just a list of jobs, is it?</v>

68
00:08:29.782 --> 00:08:40.628
<v Maya>Oh, no. The DOT was a massive physical government manual. Government assessors literally went out with clipboards and graded occupations on highly specific physical and mental requirements.</v>

69
00:08:41.028 --> 00:08:47.192
<v Daniel>And ALM realized they could use those hyper-specific DOT variables as proxies for their two-by-two matrix.</v>

70
00:08:47.812 --> 00:09:01.543
<v Maya>Exactly. Instead of just looking at the title manager, they looked at a DOT variable called DCP, which measured the requirement for direction, control, and planning. That became their proxy for interactive and managerial tasks.</v>

71
00:09:02.104 --> 00:09:06.367
<v Daniel>So they mapped these specific traits onto decades of employment data.</v>

72
00:09:06.933 --> 00:09:17.582
<v Maya>They did. They matched these task variables to census and current population survey data across 140 different industries, going all the way back to 1960 and up to 1998.</v>

73
00:09:18.263 --> 00:09:19.844
<v Daniel>Wow. 140 industries.</v>

74
00:09:19.984 --> 00:09:32.695
<v Maya>Yeah. And for routine manual tasks, they looked at a government variable called FingDex, grading finger dexterity. For non-routine manual work, they tracked eye-hand for eye-hand-foot coordination.</v>

75
00:09:33.196 --> 00:09:34.357
<v Daniel>And what about the math side?</v>

76
00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:44.570
<v Maya>For non-routine analytic tasks, they used GED math, a variable tracking advanced math requirements, and STS for routine cognitive skills.</v>

77
00:09:45.271 --> 00:09:54.160
<v Daniel>So they essentially overlaid this map of physical and mental traits onto 40 years of the American workforce. What were the headline numbers when they finally ran the data?</v>

78
00:09:55.120 --> 00:10:06.543
<v Maya>The trend lines were dramatic. During the 1960s, the input of routine tasks in the economy was actually rising. But starting in the 1970s, as computerization began, it fell sharply.</v>

79
00:10:07.243 --> 00:10:08.464
<v Daniel>And I imagine that just sped up.</v>

80
00:10:08.704 --> 00:10:21.227
<v Maya>Oh, yeah. That decline accelerated in the 80s and 90s. Conversely, non-routine analytic and interactive inputs surged after 1970. The industries that computerized the fastest saw the most dramatic shifts.</v>

81
00:10:21.847 --> 00:10:25.413
<v Daniel>And they put a hard number on how this impacted the demand for educated workers, right?</v>

82
00:10:25.633 --> 00:10:34.007
<v Maya>They did. They found that computer-induced task shifts explained about 30 to 40 percent of the total demand shift toward college-educated labor between 1970 and 1998.</v>

83
00:10:35.682 --> 00:10:36.703
<v Daniel>That's a huge chunk.</v>

84
00:10:37.023 --> 00:10:50.877
<v Maya>It is. And I should note, just to be perfectly transparent with the literature, when the final version of this paper was published in the Quarterly Journal of Economics in 2003, they refined their methodology and cited an even larger share, around 60 percent.</v>

85
00:10:51.117 --> 00:10:51.478
<v Daniel>Oh, wow.</v>

86
00:10:51.858 --> 00:10:59.826
<v Maya>But even sticking to the more conservative 30 to 40 percent range from the 2001 draft, it proves a massive structural rewiring of the economy.</v>

87
00:11:00.373 --> 00:11:05.315
<v Daniel>But the most shocking revelation to me wasn't the overall shift. It was where the shift was happening.</v>

88
00:11:05.635 --> 00:11:06.736
<v Maya>The intensive margin.</v>

89
00:11:07.216 --> 00:11:12.318
<v Daniel>Yes. Explain the intensive margin, because this is where the whole concept of a job title basically falls apart.</v>

90
00:11:12.650 --> 00:11:24.759
<v Maya>ALM discovered that more than half of this massive task change wasn't happening because people were changing careers across different occupations. It was happening within nominally identical occupations.</v>

91
00:11:24.939 --> 00:11:25.899
<v Daniel>Inside the Lego box.</v>

92
00:11:26.260 --> 00:11:41.011
<v Maya>Exactly. They proved this by comparing the DOT manual from 1977 to the updated manual from 1991. The job title of secretary in 1991 required a completely different bundle of tasks than a secretary</v>

93
00:11:41.191 --> 00:11:46.396
<v Maya>in 1977. The title was identical, but the daily reality of the job had transformed.</v>

94
00:11:46.916 --> 00:11:58.786
<v Daniel>OK, I have to play devil's advocate here, because if you're building a whole macroeconomic theory on this, we have to acknowledge the foundation is a bit shaky. The DOT data is notoriously flawed, isn't it?</v>

95
00:11:59.307 --> 00:12:00.788
<v Maya>It definitely has its issues.</v>

96
00:12:01.238 --> 00:12:12.661
<v Daniel>Yeah, I mean, those government assessors with their clipboards were heavily focused on legacy manufacturing jobs. They were notoriously terrible at sampling the service sector, which is exactly where the modern economy was actually growing.</v>

97
00:12:13.121 --> 00:12:13.681
<v Maya>That's true.</v>

98
00:12:14.041 --> 00:12:23.664
<v Daniel>Plus, there is a massive endogeneity problem here. Did the cheap computers cause the task shift, or did companies want to change their task mix and therefore bought the computers?</v>

99
00:12:24.157 --> 00:12:39.126
<v Maya>Those are entirely valid critiques. And it's important to note the ALM authors themselves explicitly acknowledged them in the paper. They admitted the DOT had imprecise definitions and sluggish updating. Right. They knew the data at the time couldn't</v>

100
00:12:39.406 --> 00:12:43.188
<v Maya>perfectly untangle that chicken and egg problem of computer investment.</v>

101
00:12:43.489 --> 00:12:50.913
<v Daniel>So if the underlying government data was that clunky, how did the core task theory survive the next two decades without collapsing under scrutiny?</v>

102
00:12:51.492 --> 00:12:59.341
<v Maya>Because economists spent the next 20 years stress testing the framework with vastly superior data and the core insight held up beautifully.</v>

103
00:12:59.662 --> 00:13:00.202
<v Daniel>For example?</v>

104
00:13:00.903 --> 00:13:10.415
<v Maya>Well, let's look at David Otter and David Dorn's 2013 paper in the American Economic Review. They use this exact routine task framework to explain job polarization.</v>

105
00:13:10.786 --> 00:13:15.891
<v Daniel>Job polarization, meaning the middle of the labor market hollows out while the extremes grow.</v>

106
00:13:16.212 --> 00:13:25.762
<v Maya>Exactly. And you see this starkly in urban centers like New York. The solid middle class routine jobs, the factory floors, the back office clerical pools, they get automated away.</v>

107
00:13:26.325 --> 00:13:32.006
<v Daniel>Leaving growth at the high wage, non-routine cognitive tail like your data scientists and corporate lawyers.</v>

108
00:13:32.267 --> 00:13:45.950
<v Maya>Right. And massive growth at the low wage, non-routine manual tail. They proved that local labor markets that historically specialized in routine work saw a huge rise in low skill in-person service jobs. Food service, cleaning, personal care.</v>

109
00:13:46.595 --> 00:13:50.939
<v Daniel>Jobs that computers couldn't do because of Polanyi's paradox, but that don't pay well.</v>

110
00:13:51.480 --> 00:14:06.454
<v Maya>Exactly. But researchers also found ways to bypass the flawed DOT data entirely. In a 2020 paper in the AEJ Applied Economics Journal, Atale, Feng-Ting Tham, Sotelo, and Tannenbaum found a brilliant workaround.</v>

111
00:14:06.855 --> 00:14:07.315
<v Daniel>What did they do?</v>

112
00:14:08.003 --> 00:14:19.470
<v Maya>They realized they didn't need government clipboards. They just needed to read what employers were asking for. They ran text analysis on local classified newspaper job ads from 1950 all the way to 2000. That</v>

113
00:14:19.530 --> 00:14:34.426
<v Daniel>is so smart. Think about a classified ad for a secretary in 1950 versus 1990. In 1950, it says wanted. Secretary able to type 60 words per minute and manage filing cabinets. Right. By 1990, the ad for the exact</v>

114
00:14:34.466 --> 00:14:44.409
<v Daniel>same title says wanted administrative assistant to manage client schedules and coordinate vendor relationships. You can physically see the Lego bricks changing in the text of the newspaper.</v>

115
00:14:44.938 --> 00:14:59.623
<v Maya>Exactly. And the 2020 study found that the shift away from routine tasks was actually larger than ALM originally measured. Crucially, the newspaper data confirmed that a massive portion of this shift occurred on that intensive margin inside those</v>

116
00:14:59.683 --> 00:15:01.023
<v Maya>narrowly defined job titles.</v>

117
00:15:01.739 --> 00:15:07.281
<v Daniel>So the ALM insight was absolutely correct. It was just undermeasured by the clumsy 1970s data.</v>

118
00:15:07.721 --> 00:15:07.941
<v Maya>Yes.</v>

119
00:15:08.641 --> 00:15:23.587
<v Daniel>Here's where it gets really interesting, though, because it's not just a story of destruction. In a 2015 paper called Why Are There Still So Many Jobs, Autor revisits the ATM paradox we talked about at the very beginning. The ATM strips the routine cash-counting bricks out of the</v>

120
00:15:23.607 --> 00:15:24.627
<v Daniel>bank teller's Lego box.</v>

121
00:15:25.077 --> 00:15:38.223
<v Maya>But by automating the routine work, ATMs made it radically cheaper for banks to open new branches. Because it was cheaper, banks opened branches everywhere. And they needed humans to staff them. They actually hired more tellers.</v>

122
00:15:38.883 --> 00:15:47.547
<v Daniel>Right. But the job had evolved into non-routine, interactive tasks. Relationship management, selling mortgages, solving complex customer problems.</v>

123
00:15:48.240 --> 00:16:02.088
<v Maya>The technology complemented the human, bounded by Polanyi's paradox. The machine does the routine math. The human handles the non-routine emotional intelligence. It's a neat, elegant theory that survived the era of spreadsheets and the internet.</v>

124
00:16:03.029 --> 00:16:13.595
<v Daniel>But it is not 2015 anymore. We are looking at 2026 data now. Does this framework survive the modern era of generative AI and large language models?</v>

125
00:16:14.400 --> 00:16:28.950
<v Maya>This is where the foundation of the original theory takes a massive hit. The strongest challenge comes from a 2023 and 2024 body of work by Alanudu, Manning, Mishkin, and Rock. They looked at large language models, or</v>

126
00:16:29.171 --> 00:16:29.651
<v Maya>LLMs.</v>

127
00:16:30.431 --> 00:16:31.192
<v Daniel>And what did they find?</v>

128
00:16:31.832 --> 00:16:42.200
<v Maya>What they found is that LLMs are now reaching directly into the higher-wage, cognitive, non-routine work that the ALM framework assumed was completely shielded from automation.</v>

129
00:16:42.682 --> 00:16:57.087
<v Daniel>Because we have to go back to my recipe analogy. Traditional computing was giving the machine a strict routine recipe. But machine learning is different. Machine learning is like locking a chef in a room with 10 million beautifully baked cakes</v>

130
00:16:57.787 --> 00:16:58.987
<v Daniel>and telling them to figure it out.</v>

131
00:16:59.888 --> 00:17:07.210
<v Maya>They don't need the explicit step-by-step rules anymore. They learn by finding patterns in massive oceans of data.</v>

132
00:17:07.826 --> 00:17:22.627
<v Daniel>They learn tacit knowledge from examples. And because of that, Polanyi's paradox is rapidly eroding. The machine doesn't need us to know how we do something. It just needs a large enough data set of humans doing it to replicate the output.</v>

133
00:17:23.152 --> 00:17:37.434
<v Maya>If it has read 10 million legal briefs, it can generate a new one, even without a rulebook on how to be persuasive. This raises an important question. Is routine still the right dividing line when the machine no longer needs</v>

134
00:17:37.514 --> 00:17:39.995
<v Maya>explicit human-coded rules?</v>

135
00:17:40.835 --> 00:17:42.635
<v Daniel>Which shatters the two-by-two matrix.</v>

136
00:17:43.315 --> 00:17:51.977
<v Maya>It really does. That is the crisis point for the 2001 theory. The routine versus non-routine mapping does not cleanly align with AI exposure today.</v>

137
00:17:52.292 --> 00:18:03.760
<v Daniel>I'm looking at the 2026 study by Chen, Fong, Wang, and Yang. They analyzed a mind-boggling 750 million Chinese job ads to track the explosion of AI.</v>

138
00:18:04.160 --> 00:18:05.281
<v Maya>And the findings are striking.</v>

139
00:18:05.821 --> 00:18:20.415
<v Daniel>Yeah. At first glance, if you look at their data and only count exposed occupations, it looks like a bloodbath. It looks like whole categories of jobs are just being wiped off the map by language models. But if I apply our Lego theory to this 2026 data,</v>

140
00:18:21.115 --> 00:18:27.660
<v Daniel>I'm guessing the occupations themselves aren't entirely vanishing. They are just shedding exposed tasks rapidly.</v>

141
00:18:28.480 --> 00:18:40.508
<v Maya>You've hit the nail on the head. When Chen and their colleagues looked inside the ads to count the tasks employers were actually asking for, they found far less was vanishing. The tasks outlived the jobs that carried them.</v>

142
00:18:41.077 --> 00:18:48.947
<v Daniel>The label on the box might change or merge with another box, but the human-centric Lego bricks inside just get recombined into new roles.</v>

143
00:18:49.433 --> 00:19:04.181
<v Maya>Right. The 2026 evidence definitely complicates the old routine mapping. But the core defining insight of the ALM paper that we must think in tasks, not jobs, because the vast majority of economic disruption happens inside the job title,</v>

144
00:19:04.782 --> 00:19:10.845
<v Maya>that remains the absolute organizing principle of the economic debate today. They got the mechanism exactly right.</v>

145
00:19:11.287 --> 00:19:26.140
<v Daniel>So what does this all mean for you as you listen to this? Let's bring this down to earth. If you are operating in the higher education landscape, specifically somewhere like the CUNY system in New York, how do you apply this? You are trying to build the academic programs of the future.</v>

146
00:19:27.041 --> 00:19:30.424
<v Daniel>You are trying to figure out what arguments and evidence explain where we are heading.</v>

147
00:19:31.118 --> 00:19:45.746
<v Maya>If we connect this to the bigger picture, the imperative for university strategy is monumental. If the routine part of every single job is the part being automated, whether by a spreadsheet in 1995 or an LLM in 2026, universities must</v>

148
00:19:45.826 --> 00:19:49.788
<v Maya>figure out how to teach and measure the remaining non-routine skills.</v>

149
00:19:50.349 --> 00:20:00.374
<v Daniel>You cannot just train someone to be a bookkeeper if the bookkeeping part is handled by an algorithm. You have to train them for the critical thinking and anomaly detection that surrounds the software.</v>

150
00:20:01.215 --> 00:20:16.185
<v Maya>Universities have to learn to credential task bundles rather than traditional job titles, and they cannot do that in a vacuum on campus. They have to partner directly and deeply with employers to see how jobs are changing from the inside in real</v>

151
00:20:16.205 --> 00:20:16.425
<v Maya>time.</v>

152
00:20:16.778 --> 00:20:27.267
<v Daniel>Because as the 1991 secretary and the 2026 Chinese job ads showed us, the job title on paper is a lagging indicator. It won't tell you the truth until it's too late.</v>

153
00:20:27.628 --> 00:20:28.208
<v Maya>Exactly.</v>

154
00:20:28.448 --> 00:20:42.701
<v Daniel>And we really have to think about who is most vulnerable here. If you are an early career worker, an adult learner, or a first generation student, which describes a huge portion of the CUNY student body, you usually enter the workforce on the bottom rungs of the ladder.</v>

155
00:20:43.093 --> 00:20:46.614
<v Maya>And traditionally, those entry-level roles are heavy on routine tasks.</v>

156
00:20:46.694 --> 00:20:57.338
<v Daniel>Right. That is literally how you learn the ropes of an industry. You do the grunt work. If those routine entry-level tasks are the most exposed to automation, how do these populations get a foothold in the economy?</v>

157
00:20:57.809 --> 00:21:11.302
<v Maya>The stakes for accurately mapping these task shifts are incredibly high for those specific populations. If higher ed doesn't adjust to credentialing the new task bundles, those students will be trained for a version of the economy that no longer exists.</v>

158
00:21:11.802 --> 00:21:26.781
<v Daniel>It brings us back to where we started. We all want a clean, simple diagnosis. We want a university degree to be a permanent certification that you are qualified for a specific job. But if that job title is now just a hollow shell hiding rapidly mutating tasks,</v>

159
00:21:27.462 --> 00:21:38.022
<v Daniel>will the university credential of the future have to look less like a one-time static diploma and more like a continuous software update? Something to mull over as you build the curriculum of tomorrow.</v>
